<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    decbench: the copy-shadow of an unmodified incoming parameter is emitted
    OUTSIDE the function's entry basic block.
      Case: O0-e2fsprogs-e2fsck-save_output  (docs/decbench/triage/)

    Source (gcc -O0 -fno-pie -fcf-protection=none -c), the guard cascade the
    triage case reduces to:

        int f(char *a, char *b)
        {
          if (a) { if (*a == 0) a = 0; }
          if (b) { if (*b == 0) b = 0; }
          return (a != 0) + (b != 0);
        }

    gcc gives each parameter a stack home and writes NULL through the parameter
    inside its guard, so the guard's join carries a MULTIEQUAL whose incoming
    slots are the raw input register:

        0:  push rbp ; mov rsp,rbp
        4:  mov rdi,-0x8(rbp) ; mov rsi,-0x10(rbp)     both spills, entry block
        c:  cmpq 0x0,-0x8(rbp) ; je 26
       13:  mov -0x8(rbp),rax ; movzbl (rax),eax ; test al,al ; jne 26
       1e:  movq 0x0,-0x8(rbp)
       26:  cmpq 0x0,-0x10(rbp) ; je 40                join of a's guard
       2d:  mov -0x10(rbp),rax ; movzbl (rax),eax ; test al,al ; jne 40
       38:  movq 0x0,-0x10(rbp)
       40:  the two setne reads of both slots

    Root cause (P6, upstream-faithful).  Merge::mergeOp cannot merge the
    input-register HighVariable with the address-tied stack-slot HighVariable, so
    Merge::trimOpInput snips each offending MULTIEQUAL slot into a COPY and places
    it with opInsertEnd(copyop, the slot's PREDECESSOR block).  That COPY is what
    prints as "vN = aM;".  For the FIRST parameter the guard's join predecessors
    intersect at the entry block, so Merge::buildDominantCopy's findCommonBlock
    lands the copy there and it renders correctly; for every later parameter the
    intersection is the previous guard's join, so the copy sinks below that guard
    and splits the source's single entry block into two.  kuna is internally
    inconsistent about the identical construct and upstream Ghidra sinks it the
    same way, so this ships as an option rather than as a bug fix.

    Stage model: P6 "merge-aggressiveness" (Merge / HighVariable formation).
    The P0 assertion "option paramcopyhoist on|off" selects it; the hoist is
    admitted only when the buildDominantCopy Cover test accepts the widened range.

    Pass 1 (option paramcopyhoist off, the default) reproduces the gap: "v1 = a1;"
    is emitted after the first guard, not with "v2 = a0;" at the top.
    Pass 2 (option paramcopyhoist on) proves the fix: both copy-shadows sit in the
    entry block, ahead of every guard.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x101000">
554889e548897df8488975f048837df8007413488b45f80fb60084c0750848c7
45f80000000048837df0007413488b45f00fb60084c0750848c745f000000000
48837df8000f95c00fb6d048837df0000f95c00fb6c001d05dc3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x101000" name="f"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>option paramcopyhoist off</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option paramcopyhoist on</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="paramcopyhoist #1: option off sinks the second parameter's copy-shadow below the first guard (gap reproduced)" min="1" max="1">v2 = NULL;
v1 = a1;</stringmatch>
